10
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:25,200
Here's an example of
a question that I've

11
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,210
got in the past
with Assignment 8.

12
00:00:27,210 --> 00:00:29,430
"Why is it that

13
00:00:29,430 --> 00:00:33,315
the read function count
value equals 131072."

14
00:00:33,315 --> 00:00:35,115
Looks like a weird number.

15
00:00:35,115 --> 00:00:36,930
Well, if you punch that into

16
00:00:36,930 --> 00:00:38,520
a calculator and convert the 0x,

17
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:41,260
you'll see that that's 0x20000.

18
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:43,760
The answer is, I don't

19
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:45,695
exactly know where
that comes from,

20
00:00:45,695 --> 00:00:47,960
but it's probably the
default buffer size of

21
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,760
whatever underlying
system call is

22
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:53,635
happening as a part
of the echo command.

23
00:00:53,635 --> 00:00:56,360
The easy place to see
where this is coming

24
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:59,300
in is again using [inaudible].

25
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:01,760
If we look at this
simple example of

26
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:04,595
writing hi to your
/dev/aesdchar,

27
00:01:04,595 --> 00:01:08,770
you can see that the read

28
00:01:08,770 --> 00:01:10,355
that's happening on

29
00:01:10,355 --> 00:01:12,800
the file descriptor
here that we opened

30
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:17,040
for dev/aesdchar is using

31
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:23,825
a 131072 parameter as the
request size for the read.

32
00:01:23,825 --> 00:01:25,595
This is again just like

33
00:01:25,595 --> 00:01:28,060
some default that's
coming in from userspace.

34
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:30,510
That would show you why that

35
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:32,760
value's showing up in
your kernel driver.

36
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:34,010
It's because it was issued by

37
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:36,275
the read command from userspace.

38
00:01:36,275 --> 00:01:38,180
Here you can see that
you actually return

39
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:40,010
three bytes from your driver,

40
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:41,600
so your driver
worked successfully

41
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:43,010
in that it said, well,

42
00:01:43,010 --> 00:01:45,185
I don't have this
many bytes to return,

43
00:01:45,185 --> 00:01:46,710
but I do have three
bytes to return,

44
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:48,200
so I'll return those bytes here

45
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:51,535
and set that value
as my return value.

46
00:01:51,535 --> 00:01:53,870
Similarly, you can see that

47
00:01:53,870 --> 00:01:55,715
there's another
pull that happens.

48
00:01:55,715 --> 00:01:58,760
After you've read
those three bytes out,

49
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:00,500
userspace pulls again to

50
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,420
see if there's more data
available and again,

51
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:05,755
it just asks for that
chunk size of 131072.

52
00:02:05,755 --> 00:02:07,280
In this case, your
driver's saying,

53
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:08,960
I don't have anything
else because this was

54
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:11,450
the only thing that
was written so far

55
00:02:11,450 --> 00:02:13,040
and so it's just
going to return zero

56
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:16,650
and not fill anything
into the buffer.

57
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:23,820
Here's another case where
a student has said, "Well,

58
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:26,135
my aesd read function
is just getting,

59
00:02:26,135 --> 00:02:27,710
I can turn on [inaudible]
and I see that

60
00:02:27,710 --> 00:02:30,040
it's continuously called
over and over again."

61
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:33,290
Most likely the reason why
this is happening is because

62
00:02:33,290 --> 00:02:36,845
you aren't handling
the return value

63
00:02:36,845 --> 00:02:38,585
of your read function correctly.

64
00:02:38,585 --> 00:02:40,460
You need to remember to

65
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:42,440
return zero to say
that there's no data

66
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:44,210
remaining to be
read after you've

67
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:46,000
finished all the
content of your buffer.

68
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,470
Because otherwise, the userspace

69
00:02:48,470 --> 00:02:49,970
is just going to be
keep calling you

70
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:54,275
like this to wait
until you return zero.

71
00:02:54,275 --> 00:02:56,300
If you return something
other than zero,

72
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:58,055
it's going to think you
have more data to return

73
00:02:58,055 --> 00:03:00,370
as long as you keep
returning that value.

74
00:03:00,370 --> 00:03:03,200
This gets into the read return
values discussion that we

75
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,850
had earlier and the rules
about what your return.

76
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:06,890
You need to know that at

77
00:03:06,890 --> 00:03:07,940
the end of the
file where there's

78
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:10,865
no data available to be
read into the buffer,

79
00:03:10,865 --> 00:03:13,620
you need to return
zero in this case.

80
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:17,330
Another question,
depending on the design

81
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:19,520
of your aesd socket
implementation,

82
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:21,665
maybe use fseek or stat,

83
00:03:21,665 --> 00:03:23,585
try to find the size of

84
00:03:23,585 --> 00:03:26,635
the file as part of
your implementation.

85
00:03:26,635 --> 00:03:28,580
It turns out that's not
really going to work

86
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:30,935
with our device driver
implementation.

87
00:03:30,935 --> 00:03:33,275
fseek is not going to
work because we don't

88
00:03:33,275 --> 00:03:35,790
implement the necessary
write seek support.

89
00:03:35,790 --> 00:03:38,040
If you remember, we said,

90
00:03:38,040 --> 00:03:40,210
we're doing in a simpler
implementation of write

91
00:03:40,210 --> 00:03:42,380
that just ignores
the seek parameter.

92
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:43,940
You need to implement that in

93
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:45,970
order for fseek
to work properly.

94
00:03:45,970 --> 00:03:50,090
Stat doesn't work because
dev aesdchar is not a file,

95
00:03:50,090 --> 00:03:52,580
so it's not really going
to be possible for you to

96
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:56,205
determine the size of the dev,

97
00:03:56,205 --> 00:03:59,910
at least not easily
to get the size of

98
00:03:59,910 --> 00:04:02,755
the dev from aesdsocket

99
00:04:02,755 --> 00:04:05,965
the same way that you
would with a real file.

100
00:04:05,965 --> 00:04:08,900
What I would suggest instead was

101
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:11,720
just remove the logic
that requires a stat and

102
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:13,850
just read and write to

103
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:16,010
the file descriptor in
a loop until you get

104
00:04:16,010 --> 00:04:18,320
